02:09:01.000 --> 02:09:03.560
You'll define some thing about the optimizer here.

02:09:03.560 --> 02:09:04.760
In our case, it's zero grad.

02:09:04.760 --> 02:09:08.840
And then you'll have a loss dot backward,

02:09:08.840 --> 02:09:12.360
which is backward pass and the optimizer dot step,

02:09:12.360 --> 02:09:15.000
which lets gradient descent work.

02:09:15.000 --> 02:09:15.560
It's magic.

02:09:16.280 --> 02:09:19.720
So back to optimizer does zero grad.

02:09:19.720 --> 02:09:27.080
So by default pie torch will accumulate the gradients over time via adding them.

02:09:28.520 --> 02:09:31.000
And what we do by,

02:09:31.000 --> 02:09:34.440
by putting zero grad is we make sure that they do not add over time.

02:09:34.440 --> 02:09:37.880
So the previous gradients do not affect the current one.

02:09:38.840 --> 02:09:43.240
And the reason we don't want this is because previous gradients are from previous data

02:09:43.800 --> 02:09:47.480
and the data is, you know, kind of weird sometimes.

02:09:47.480 --> 02:09:52.200
Sometimes it's biased and we don't want that determining, you know,

02:09:52.200 --> 02:09:55.400
how much like what our error is, right?

02:09:55.960 --> 02:09:58.920
So we only want to decide, we only want to optimize

02:09:58.920 --> 02:10:01.640
based on the current gradient of our current data.

02:10:01.640 --> 02:10:06.200
And this little parameter in here, we go set to none.

02:10:06.200 --> 02:10:07.880
This pretty much means we're going to set,

02:10:09.960 --> 02:10:12.280
we're going to set the gradients instead of zero.

02:10:12.280 --> 02:10:15.560
Instead of zero gradient, we're going to set it to none.

02:10:16.120 --> 02:10:20.280
And the reason why we set it to none is because none occupies a lot less space.

02:10:22.040 --> 02:10:25.080
It just, yeah, it just occupies a lot less space when you have a zero.

02:10:25.080 --> 02:10:29.080
That's probably an int 64 or something that's going to take up space.

02:10:29.080 --> 02:10:34.840
And because, you know, we might have a lot of these accumulating that takes up space over time.

02:10:34.840 --> 02:10:39.160
So we want to make sure that the set to none is true.

02:10:39.160 --> 02:10:41.880
At least for this case, sometimes you might not want to.

02:10:42.600 --> 02:10:46.280
And that's pretty much what that does.

02:10:47.000 --> 02:10:52.680
It will, if you do have a zero grad on commonly,

02:10:52.680 --> 02:10:57.400
the only reason you'll need it is for training large recurrent neural nets,

02:10:57.400 --> 02:11:02.440
which need to understand previous context because they're recurrent.

02:11:02.440 --> 02:11:05.000
I'm not going to dive into RNNs right now,

02:11:05.000 --> 02:11:10.040
but those are a big use case for not having zero grad.

02:11:10.040 --> 02:11:15.160
Gradient accumulation will simply take an average of all the accumulation steps

02:11:15.800 --> 02:11:18.520
and just averages the gradients together.

02:11:18.520 --> 02:11:22.520
So you get a more effective, maybe block size, right?

02:11:22.520 --> 02:11:26.760
You get more context that way, and you can have the same batch size.

02:11:26.760 --> 02:11:29.080
So just little neat tricks like that.

02:11:29.080 --> 02:11:32.920
We'll talk about gradient accumulation more later in the course,

02:11:33.720 --> 02:11:35.560
but pretty much what's going on here.

