You can look into that more if you'd like,

02:14:45.160 --> 02:14:48.360
but that's our most basic optimizer.

02:14:48.360 --> 02:14:50.520
Gradient descent is a step up from that.

02:14:51.080 --> 02:14:53.400
It's used to minimize the loss function in a model,

02:14:54.200 --> 02:14:55.480
measures how well the model,

02:14:56.120 --> 02:14:59.880
the gradient measures how well the model is able to predict

02:14:59.880 --> 02:15:02.520
the target variable based on the input features.

02:15:02.520 --> 02:15:04.200
So we have some input X,

02:15:04.200 --> 02:15:07.960
we have some weights and biases, maybe WX plus B.

02:15:08.840 --> 02:15:14.280
And all we're trying to do is make sure that the inputs

02:15:14.920 --> 02:15:21.880
or make sure that we make the inputs become the desired outputs.

02:15:21.880 --> 02:15:26.040
And based on how far it is away from the desired outputs,

02:15:26.040 --> 02:15:28.600
we can change the parameters of the model.

02:15:28.600 --> 02:15:32.440
So we were over gradient descent recently or previously,

02:15:32.440 --> 02:15:34.920
but that's pretty much what's going on here.

02:15:36.040 --> 02:15:40.840
And momentum is just a little extension of gradient descent

02:15:41.800 --> 02:15:43.640
that adds the momentum term.

02:15:44.280 --> 02:15:46.200
So it helps smooth out the training

02:15:47.240 --> 02:15:51.400
and allows it to continue moving in the right direction,

02:15:52.040 --> 02:15:55.080
even if the gradient changes direction or varies magnitude.

02:15:55.080 --> 02:15:58.040
It's particularly useful for training deep neural nets.

02:15:58.040 --> 02:16:01.560
So momentum is when you have, you know,

02:16:01.560 --> 02:16:05.320
you consider some of the other gradients.

02:16:05.320 --> 02:16:07.640
So you have something that's like maybe passed on from here

02:16:08.200 --> 02:16:11.080
and then it might include a little bit of the current one.

02:16:11.080 --> 02:16:14.440
So like 90%, like a good momentum coefficient

02:16:14.440 --> 02:16:17.320
would be like 90% previous gradients

02:16:17.320 --> 02:16:19.560
and then 10% of the current one.

02:16:19.560 --> 02:16:21.560
So it kind of like lags behind

02:16:21.560 --> 02:16:23.800
and makes it converge sort of smoothly.

02:16:23.800 --> 02:16:24.440
That makes sense.

02:16:24.440 --> 02:16:26.840
RMS prop, I've never used this,

02:16:26.840 --> 02:16:29.640
but it's an algorithm that used the moving average

02:16:29.640 --> 02:16:31.000
of the squared gradient

02:16:31.000 --> 02:16:33.000
to adapt the learning rates of each parameter.

02:16:33.000 --> 02:16:35.880
Helps to avoid oscillations in the parameter updates

02:16:35.880 --> 02:16:38.840
and can improve convergence in some cases.

02:16:38.840 --> 02:16:40.680
So you can look more into that if you'd like.

02:16:41.640 --> 02:16:43.560
Atom, very popular,

02:16:44.200 --> 02:16:46.680
combines the ideas of momentum and RMS prop.

02:16:47.800 --> 02:16:49.480
It uses a moving average,

02:16:49.480 --> 02:16:51.400
both the gradient and its squared value

02:16:51.400 --> 02:16:54.520
to adapt the learning rate of each parameter.

02:16:54.520 --> 02:16:57.000
It's often used as the default optimizer

02:16:57.000 --> 02:16:58.520
for deep learning models.

02:16:58.520 --> 02:17:02.040
And in our case, when we continue to build this out,

02:17:02.040 --> 02:17:04.760
it's going to be quite a deep net.

02:17:04.760 --> 02:17:09.080
And Atom W is just a modification of the Atom optimizer

02:17:09.080 --> 02:17:11.320
that adds weight decay to the parameter updates.

02:17:11.320 --> 02:17:13.560
So helps to regularize

02:17:13.560 --> 02:17:15.880
and it can prove generalization performance.

